From: libvlc-wasm <noreply@libvlc-wasm>
Subject: [PATCH] player: A-B loop deadline: integer ticks, divide by the rate

vlc_player_GetAtoBLoopDeadline() returned `now + (b_time - ts) * rate` with a
float rate, so the whole sum was computed in float. A float's step at 2^50 is
2^27, so where vlc_tick_now() counts from the Unix epoch (emscripten's
CLOCK_MONOTONIC is performance.timeOrigin-based, ~1.8e15 us) the deadline was
rounded to a 134 s grid: the loop fired tens of seconds early -- seeking back
to A continuously -- or late. Clocks counting from boot hide it at ms scale.

It also multiplied the remaining media time by the rate where it must divide
(at 2x the loop fired late, at 0.5x early).
---
--- a/src/player/player.c
+++ b/src/player/player.c
@@ -213,7 +213,10 @@
             return VLC_TICK_MIN;
         b_time = input->abloop_state[1].pos * input->length;
     }
-    return now + (b_time - ts) * input->rate;
+    /* Media time runs `rate` times faster than the system clock. Keep the
+     * sum in integer ticks: `now + float` is computed in float, which has a
+     * 2^27 us (134 s) step at an epoch-based tick like emscripten's. */
+    return now + (vlc_tick_t)((b_time - ts) / input->rate);
 }
 
 static void *
